<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px; margin-bottom: 20px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0; font-size: 2em;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Our Amazing Community – Lesson # 5</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 5</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Mediation</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 6th Grade (CEFR: A2.1)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Our Amazing Community</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> This Is My Amazing Community.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Mediation: text: Simplify complex instructions for classmates. concept: Use diagrams to explain concepts to peers.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Mediation: Can simplify complex instructions for classmates, using visuals (e.g., draw pictures on a map to indicate the type of building). Can use diagrams to simplify community topics for peers (e.g., draw a map of the community and label main areas). Can explain facts about familiar roles in the community using simple diagrams and words (e.g., shows a drawing of a classroom and says, 'Teachers in our school help students learn.').</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 30px;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 33%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 33%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 33%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher greets students and asks, "What is one important job in our community?" Students quickly share one job (e.g., "A teacher!"). The teacher shows flashcards of various community roles (e.g., teacher, farmer, police officer, vendor, artist, carpenter) and asks students to name them. For each card, the teacher prompts: "What does a [job] do?" Students respond with simple sentences. This activates prior knowledge and vocabulary from previous lessons. For example, "A farmer grows fresh food."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher holds up a flashcard for 'teacher' and says, "This is a teacher. A teacher helps students learn. I have worked as a teacher for five years." The teacher then draws a simple stick figure of a teacher writing on a board and says, "See? This diagram shows what a teacher does. It makes it easy to understand." The teacher emphasizes the stress in "police officer". The teacher explicitly models how to simplify information and use a visual.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Do we want to make things easy or difficult to understand today?" (Expected answer: Easy!)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Can we use drawings to help explain?" (Expected answer: Yes!)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Our Amazing Community".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> The teacher presents two short sets of instructions for a simple community event (e.g., "How to organize a small park cleanup"). One set of instructions is slightly more complex (e.g., uses longer sentences, more detailed steps), and the other is a simplified version with bullet points and small, clear diagrams/icons next to each step. The instructions include vocabulary like 'volunteer', 'gather', 'share', 'park', 'event'.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Students will learn to interpret and simplify informational text and convey its essence using visuals and simple language for a peer.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher first reads the complex instructions aloud. Students listen for key words.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist question:</b> "What is the main idea of these instructions?" (Expected answer: How to clean the park.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher then shows the simplified instructions with diagrams. "Which instructions are easier to understand? Why?" Students discuss how diagrams help.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False:</b> The teacher makes statements based on the simplified instructions, e.g., "We need to bring our own trash bags." (True/False based on the text). This also identifies short and long vowel sounds in 'cook' and 'school' if related words are used.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Mediation.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Simplifying information using present simple tense, new vocabulary (community roles, verbs, adjectives), and basic diagrams. Practicing asking questions in present simple and using time expressions.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice - Matching & Sentence Completion:</b> The teacher provides a worksheet with three slightly more complex sentences describing community roles (e.g., "A vendor sells fresh produce and other goods at the local market every day.") on one side and simple diagrams or icons representing those roles (e.g., a basket of fruit, a market stall) on the other. Students draw lines to match the sentence to the correct diagram. Below this, students complete simple sentences using the vocabulary: "A [job] ___ [action]." (e.g., "A musician ______ sweet music.") They use present simple.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Practice - Diagram Description:</b> The teacher shows 2-3 new simple diagrams of community roles (e.g., a carpenter building, a gardener watering plants). In pairs, students formulate 2-3 sentences to explain what each person does, using the present simple and relevant vocabulary. Example: "A carpenter builds things. They build houses in our neighborhood." They use 'build', 'neighborhood'. Teacher circulates, providing feedback and checking for correct present simple usage and vocabulary.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction - Question & Explain:</b> Students work in pairs. Each student chooses a community role not yet discussed (e.g., artist, musician). They prepare to explain it in 2-3 simple sentences and draw a quick diagram. Their partner asks questions using present simple: "What does an artist do?" or "What delicious food do people cook at the market?" The first student explains using their simple sentences and shows their diagram. They then switch roles. The teacher encourages use of 'can' and 'should'.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Our Amazing Community".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> "Community Role Posters" Presentation and Explanation.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students will present their "Community Role Posters" (prepared in previous lessons) to a small group of classmates. Their task is to *mediate* the information on their poster by simplifying it, using their poster's diagrams and visuals to explain the community member's role and responsibilities. They should also include one fact about their experience (present perfect) or a past community event (past simple).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students form groups of 3-4. Each student takes turns presenting their poster.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">They introduce the community member: "This is a [job]. This person has an important job in our community."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">They explain the role and responsibilities using simple sentences and pointing to visuals on their poster: "[Job]s usually [verb] [object] every day. For example, a gardener helps keep our park beautiful. They water plants."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">They integrate either a past simple or present perfect sentence: "Last month, our gardener planted new flowers." or "My father has worked as a carpenter for ten years."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">After each presentation, group members can ask one question about the role or poster. (e.g., "What role do you think you play in your community?", "How long has your teacher worked here?"). The presenter simplifies any complex ideas in their answer.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher reminds students to use proper stress in compound nouns like "gardener" and clear vowel sounds for words like "cook" and "school" if used.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher Observation Checklist and Peer Assessment (simple checklist).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students will individually present their "Community Role Poster" and explain the role of the community member to the teacher or a small peer group. They must simplify the information, use their visuals effectively, and include a present simple, past simple, or present perfect sentence. Peers will use a simple checklist.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher Checklist):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarity of Explanation:</b> Explains the community role in simple, understandable English.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of Visuals/Diagrams:</b> Effectively uses poster visuals to support the explanation.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Target Vocabulary:</b> Uses at least 3-4 target vocabulary words (e.g., 'community', 'responsibilities', 'job', specific role nouns, verbs like 'share', 'build', 'cook').</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Grammar (Present Simple/Past Simple/Present Perfect):</b> Correctly uses present simple to describe the job, and either past simple or present perfect for an experience/event.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pronunciation:</b> Generally intelligible, attempts correct stress for compound nouns.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete two sentences on a slip of paper:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Today, I learned how to simplify information by ___________________."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"One community member's job I can explain easily is ___________________ because ___________________."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What part of explaining your poster was easy? What was difficult?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How can using pictures help you explain things better in English?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in helping students simplify information and use diagrams? (e.g., specific activities, types of visuals).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or scaffolds are needed for students who struggled with simplifying or using the target grammar? (e.g., more sentence frames, pre-made simple diagrams).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Did the students demonstrate understanding of community roles and responsibilities?</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> This lesson concludes the "Our Amazing Community" theme. The next lesson will introduce a new theme, possibly focusing on a different aspect of local life or culture, building on the communicative skills developed here but with new content and objectives, perhaps asking students to mediate a short text about a historical event in their community.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>